  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs high pressure becomes nearly stationary off the Mid-Atlantic \nCoast, the area will be impacted by multiple shortwaves moving along \na broader upper level trough into next week. Currently, a weak cold \nfront is slowly meandering east towards the area, however is \nexpected to stall just west of the CWA today. This should allow for \nthe bulk of the area to remain dry, although some afternoon, \ndiurnally driven showers are possible given the increased moisture \nflow and daytime instability. The better forcing looks to arrive \nlate tonight into Friday as a mid-level shortwave helps to sag the \ncold front closer to the area and allow for additional support for \nshowers. More widespread shower and thunderstorm potential will \nincrease in the afternoon with additional support from daytime \nheating, but given weak shear not expecting any severe storms. Some \nof these showers will linger into Saturday. Locally heavy rainfall \nremains the primary concern with showers that develop given a near \nparallel mean flow, expected slow storm motion, and increasing \nmoisture with dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s and PWAT values \nclimbing to 1.5\" or more. \n\nLate Saturday into Sunday, there should be a notable break in \nprecipitation chances as the area falls under a weak ridge briefly, \nbut it will be short lived as another shortwave moves along the \nbroad trough and moves another weak boundary toward/through the \narea. Widespread shower and thunderstorm chances once again increase \nSunday evening into Monday. This shortwave will quickly be followed \nby an additional one late Monday into Tuesday. With these general \nunsettled pattern expected to persist into mid-week next week, \nexpect numerous rounds of showers across the area. Showers into next \nweek will continue to have the heavy rain potential. In areas \nimpacted by multiple rounds, localized flooding will be possible. To \nhighlight the hydrology concern, WPC has at least a portion of the \narea in a Marginal ERO through Day 5. With the weak convective \nelements, SPC has only highlighted the area in general thunder. \n\nTemperatures through this entire period will linger near normal with \nhighs in the 80s and overnight lows in the 60s. With very muggy \nconditions expected, apparent temperatures may climb closer to 90 \nduring peak heating.",
